  <Fragment slot="lede">The Union of the Comoros is highly vulnerable to a wide range of natural hazards, including cyclones, floods, volcanic eruptions, and epidemics, with over 54% of its population living in high-risk areas.</Fragment>

  <!-- Country context: shown in full -->
  <Fragment slot="context">
    <p>The Union of the Comoros is highly vulnerable to a wide range of natural hazards, including cyclones, floods, volcanic eruptions, and epidemics, with over 54% of its population living in high-risk areas. Environmental degradation, particularly deforestation driven by reliance on biomass, further amplifies disaster risks. The devastating impact of Tropical Cyclone Kenneth in 2019, which affected 345,000 people and displaced over 20,000, highlighted vulnerability and critical gaps in disaster preparedness and response mechanisms.</p>
    <p>Despite efforts to establish a national disaster risk management (DRM) structure through the creation of the General Directorate of Civil Security (DGSC) and the National Platform for Disaster Risk Reduction (PNPRRC), the legal and operational frameworks remain underdeveloped, especially regarding displacement and human mobility. Local communities are insufficiently informed about climate and geological risks, and the country lacks robust systems for early warning, displacement data management, and emergency evacuation planning.</p>
  </Fragment>

